# What never reaches the build context.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the one that matters: a smaller context is a
# faster build, but a context that cannot carry a secret is a build that cannot
# bake one into a layer. Anything here is invisible to `docker build`, so a
# `.env` a developer pointed at a real cluster cannot end up in an image even by
# accident.
#
# What the build needs is the module: go.mod, go.sum, cmd/, internal/ and
# migrations/ — which is embedded, so it must stay.

# A local copy of .env.example, holding whatever the developer pointed it at.
# The example itself is kept out too: the image reads its configuration from the
# environment, never from a file in it.
.env
.env.example

# History, and the branch state that comes with it. By far the largest thing in
# the tree, and none of it is compiled.
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Local build and test artefacts, which would otherwise be copied in and then
# overwritten by the build that produced them.
/migrate
*.test
*.out

# The local stack's own definitions. The realm export and the queue init script
# are mounted into their containers by docker-compose.yml at run time; they are
# not part of any image.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
deploy/

# Documentation and the developer toolchain's configuration. Read by people and
# by CI, never by the compiler.
*.md
docs/
Makefile
.golangci.yml

# Session state for the assistant tooling, and macOS directory metadata.
.remember/
.DS_Store
